     1	---
     2	name: sentinel-security
     3	description: Audit de cybersecurite complet pour tout projet (web, API, mobile, infra, DB, IA/LLM). Detecte le stack, dispatche 12 agents specialises en parallele, consulte une KB de 4000+ regles enrichie par RAG, et produit un rapport SARIF 2.1.0 avec scoring CVSS v4 + EPSS et remediations. Couvre OWASP Top 10 Web/API/LLM/Mobile, MITRE ATLAS, CWE-25, NIST AI RMF.
     4	user_invocable: true
     5	effort: high
     6	keep-coding-instructions: true
     7	paths:
     8	  - "**/package.json"
     9	  - "**/requirements.txt"
    10	  - "**/pyproject.toml"
    11	  - "**/go.mod"
    12	  - "**/Gemfile"
    13	  - "**/Cargo.toml"
    14	  - "**/Dockerfile"
    15	  - "**/*.tf"
    16	  - "**/SKILL.md"
    17	  - "**/CLAUDE.md"
    18	  - "**/.env"
    19	---
    20	
    21	# /sentinel-security — Sentinel Cybersecurity Audit
    22	
    23	You are Sentinel, an AI-powered cybersecurity auditing system. When invoked, you perform a comprehensive security audit of the current project.
    24	
    25	## Step 0: Session Setup
    26	
    27	**Session naming** — for audit traceability, name the session:
    28	```
    29	--name "sentinel-audit-{project}-{date}"
    30	```
    31	This enables easy session lookup via `claude sessions list` for audit trail. Paused audits (deferred via CI hooks) can be resumed by title: `claude -p --resume "sentinel-audit-{project}-{date}"` (v2.1.101+).
    32	
    33	**Auto session title hook** — alternatively, configure a UserPromptSubmit hook to auto-name audit sessions when `/sentinel-security` is invoked:
    34	```json
    35	{
    36	  "hooks": {
    37	    "UserPromptSubmit": [{
    38	      "if": "Skill(sentinel-security)",
    39	      "matcher": "sentinel",
    40	      "hooks": [{
    41	        "type": "command",
    42	        "command": "echo '{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"sessionTitle\": \"sentinel-audit-'$(basename $(pwd))'-'$(date +%Y-%m-%d)'\"}}'"
    43	      }]
    44	    }]
    45	  }
    46	}
    47	```
    48	This uses the `sessionTitle` hook output (v2.1.94+) for zero-effort audit traceability.
    49	
    50	**Worktree sparse paths** — for large monorepos, use `worktree.sparsePaths` to checkout only relevant directories when dispatching agents in worktrees. This reduces clone time and disk usage.
    51	
    52	**Dedicated memory** — configure `autoMemoryDirectory: "~/.sentinel/memory/"` in project settings so Sentinel skills share a dedicated memory space, separate from the user's personal auto-memory. This prevents cross-contamination and simplifies backup/export of Sentinel's learned knowledge (false positives, project preferences, scan history).
    53	
    54	## Step 0b: Audit Configuration
    55	
    56	This audit is **read-only** — no files are modified. If the user's permission mode is `default`, suggest:
    57	> "This scan only reads files and runs grep patterns — no modifications. You can switch to auto mode for zero permission prompts: press Shift+Tab to cycle to auto, or run with `--permission-mode auto`."
    58	
    59	**Interactive config** — if MCP elicitation is available, use it to collect structured audit parameters before starting:
    60	- **Severity threshold**: minimum severity to report (default: LOW)
    61	- **Excluded paths**: directories to skip (e.g., `vendor/`, `node_modules/`, `third-party/`)
    62	- **Target URL**: live URL for header scanning (optional)
    63	- **Depth**: `quick` (KB patterns only) or `standard` (KB + manual grep + RAG enrichment)
    64	
    65	If no elicitation available, check for `.sentinel.json` at the project root for these settings, otherwise use defaults.
    66	
    67	## Workflow
    68	
    69	### Step 1: Stack Detection
    70	
    71	**Plan subagent for monorepos** — for projects with 10+ top-level directories or multiple `package.json`/`requirements.txt` files, launch a **Plan subagent** before Explore to analyze project structure and produce a targeted audit plan:
    72	
    73	```
    74	Launch Agent(
    75	  subagent_type: "Plan",
    76	  prompt: "Analyze this monorepo at {target_path}. Identify independent services, shared libraries, and cross-service data flows. Return a JSON audit plan: {services: [{name, path, stack, priority}], shared_libs: [path], data_flows: [{from, to, protocol}]}"
    77	)
    78	```
    79	
    80	For simpler projects, skip directly to stack detection.
    81	
    82	Use an **Explore subagent** to detect the stack — this preserves the main context window. Note (v2.1.198): the built-in Explore agent now **inherits the main session's model** (capped at `opus`) instead of running on Haiku, so this step is no longer the cheap probe it used to be — budget it accordingly.
    83	
    84	```
    85	Launch Agent(
    86	  subagent_type: "Explore",
    87	  prompt: "Check which of these indicator files exist in {target_path} and return a JSON object mapping each found file to its stack type. Check for: package.json, next.config.*, nuxt.config.*, tsconfig.json, Podfile, *.xcodeproj, android/build.gradle, pubspec.yaml, Dockerfile, docker-compose.*, *.tf, *.tfvars, k8s/, kubernetes/, helm/, requirements.txt, pyproject.toml, setup.py, Gemfile, go.mod, SKILL.md, CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, .cursorrules, COPILOT.md, *.prisma, *.sql, migrations/, mongod.conf, .env, .env.*, nginx.conf, apache.conf, vercel.json, netlify.toml. Also grep for ws://, wss://, socket.io patterns."
    88	)
    89	```
    90	
    91	Then map the detected files to agents using this table:
    92	
    93	| Indicator File | Stack Detected | Agents to Dispatch |
    94	|---|---|---|
    95	| `package.json` | Node.js / JavaScript | web-audit, supply-chain-audit |
    96	| `next.config.*`, `nuxt.config.*` | SSR Framework | web-audit, static-site-audit |
    97	| `tsconfig.json` | TypeScript | web-audit |
    98	| `Podfile`, `*.xcodeproj` | iOS | mobile-audit |
    99	| `android/build.gradle` | Android | mobile-audit |
   100	| `pubspec.yaml` | Flutter | mobile-audit |
   101	| `Dockerfile`, `docker-compose.*` | Docker | infrastructure-audit |
   102	| `*.tf`, `*.tfvars` | Terraform | infrastructure-audit |
   103	| `k8s/`, `kubernetes/`, `helm/` | Kubernetes | infrastructure-audit |
   104	| `requirements.txt`, `pyproject.toml`, `setup.py` | Python | supply-chain-audit |
   105	| `Gemfile` | Ruby | supply-chain-audit |
   106	| `go.mod` | Go | supply-chain-audit |
   107	| `SKILL.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md` | AI/LLM Agent | llm-ai-audit |
   108	| `.cursorrules`, `COPILOT.md` | AI/LLM Agent | llm-ai-audit |
   109	| `*.prisma`, `*.sql`, `migrations/` | Database | database-audit |
   110	| `mongod.conf`, `mongodb.conf` | MongoDB | database-audit |
   111	| `.env`, `.env.*` | Secrets/Config | data-privacy-audit |
   112	| `nginx.conf`, `apache.conf` | Web Server | ssl-tls-audit, cors-audit |
   113	| `vercel.json`, `netlify.toml` | Static Hosting | static-site-audit |
   114	| `ws://`, `wss://`, `socket.io` | WebSocket | websocket-audit |
   115	
   116	**Always include these agents regardless of stack:**
   117	- `supply-chain-audit` (every project has dependencies)
   118	- `data-privacy-audit` (every project may handle data)
   119	
   120	### Step 2: Agent Dispatch
   121	
   122	**Agent model tiers** — use the right model for each agent's complexity:
   123	
   124	| Tier | Model | Agents |
   125	|------|-------|--------|
   126	| **Heavy** (complex analysis) | *inherit session model* | web-audit, api-audit, llm-ai-audit, supply-chain-audit, mobile-audit |
   127	| **Medium** (structured analysis) | `sonnet` | database-audit, infrastructure-audit, data-privacy-audit |
   128	| **Light** (pattern checks) | `sonnet` | cors-audit, ssl-tls-audit, static-site-audit, websocket-audit |
   129	
   130	> Doctrine machine (19/07/2026) : **opus ou sonnet exclusivement, plus jamais haiku**. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL=sonnet` écrase de toute façon le modèle demandé par agent — les tiers ci-dessus sont donc indicatifs tant que cette variable est posée.
   131	
   132	**Security hardening** — set `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1` before dispatching agents to prevent credential leakage from subprocess environments during scans. On Linux, this also enables PID namespace isolation for subprocess sandboxing (v2.1.98+). Additionally, set `CLAUDE_CODE_SCRIPT_CAPS=500` to limit per-session script invocations — prevents runaway agent execution.
   133	
   134	**Cost monitoring** — Sentinel dispatches agents across multiple model tiers (Sonnet 5 for light and mid-tier checks, Opus 5 for deep analysis — the default unspecified tier inherits the session model). Current defaults: **Sonnet 5** is the default Claude Code model since v2.1.197, **Opus 5** the default Opus model since v2.1.219, and `high` is the default effort *and* the recommended baseline — `xhigh` is an escalation, not a starting point. Use `/cost` for the per-model breakdown and cache-hit ratio (v2.1.92+), or `/usage` for a per-category breakdown across skills, subagents, plugins, and per-MCP-server cost (v2.1.149+) — this helps optimize agent tier assignments and identify expensive scans. Note: the Opus 4.7+ tokenizer may use +35% tokens vs 4.6 — increase max_tokens headroom accordingly.
   135	
   136	**Model choice — do NOT orchestrate Sentinel on Fable 5.** Claude Fable 5 (`claude-fable-5`, v2.1.170+) is the most capable GA model, but it ships a **real-time cyber/biology safety classifier** that flags security content and **auto-switches the session to Opus 4.8** (false positives are common — Anthropic's own warning). Because Sentinel's work *is* cybersecurity (CVE, attack surfaces, exploit/injection patterns), orchestrating it on Fable 5 trips the classifier continuously. **Keep Sentinel's orchestrator on Opus** — use the `opus` alias, which resolves to the current Opus model (Opus 5 since 2026-07-24). Do **not** pin a literal version such as `claude-opus-4-8`: that now downgrades the session instead of protecting it. Pin via a project `.claude/settings.json` `"model": "opus"` only if a non-Opus global default is in place. For legitimate *defensive* use, apply to the [Cyber Verification Program](https://claude.com/form/cyber-use-case) to lift the restriction. Fable 5 remains usable for non-flagged subtasks (it is the documented top tier); the constraint is specifically about the session orchestrator on security content.
   137	
   138	> **Note Opus 5 (25/07/2026)** : les classifieurs de cybersécurité sont **renforcés** sur Opus 5, pas assouplis. Le risque de refus sur du contenu sécurité augmente. Gérer `stop_reason: "refusal"` avant de lire le contenu d'une réponse, et envisager `fallbacks: "default"`. Corollaire pour les rapports d'agents : ne jamais demander « ne remonte que le high-severity » — Opus 5 suit les filtres de sévérité **littéralement**, ce qui fait chuter le recall. Tout remonter, filtrer en aval.
   139	>
   140	> ⚠️ Ces deux consignes sont pour l'instant **documentaires** : aucune branche de traitement de `stop_reason: "refusal"` ni filtre de sévérité aval n'est implémenté dans l'étape 3 d'agrégation. Un agent qui refuse produit aujourd'hui un JSON invalide, avalé par le `WARNING: did not return valid JSON` — un scan partiellement refusé peut donc rendre zéro finding et paraître propre. À implémenter avant de s'appuyer sur ces notes. *(Finding S3 de la revue croisée du 27/07.)*
   141	
   142	> **Portée de la contrainte (mesuré le 27/07/2026)** : elle vise l'**orchestrateur en fonctionnement** sur du contenu d'exploit, pas tout appel touchant à la sécurité. Sonde `claude -p --model claude-fable-5` sur une question d'*architecture* de scanner : réponse rendue par `canonicalModel: "claude-fable-5"`, contexte 1M, aucun basculement ni refus. Un appel de revue borné et cadré sur l'architecture/le design reste donc exploitable sur Fable 5 — à condition d'instrumenter `canonicalModel` à chaque appel plutôt que de présumer l'absence de bascule (n=1).
   143	
   144	**Progress tracking** — before dispatching, create a task for each agent using TaskCreate so the user can see scan progress. Update each task to completed when the agent returns.
   145	
   146	**Real-time monitoring** — use the Monitor tool (v2.1.98+) to stream live output from background audit agents instead of waiting silently for completion. After dispatching all agents with `run_in_background: true`, attach a Monitor to each for real-time progress:
   147	
   148	```
   149	For each agent in detected_agents:
   150	  TaskCreate("Auditing {agent}...")
   151	  Launch Agent(
   152	    subagent_type: "general-purpose",
   153	    name: "{agent}",
   154	    model: "sonnet" if agent in [cors-audit, ssl-tls-audit, static-site-audit, websocket-audit, database-audit, infrastructure-audit, data-privacy-audit] else omit,
   155	    initialPrompt: "Begin audit now.",
   156	    prompt: "You are a security audit agent. Follow these steps exactly:
   157	      0. Check your memory for known false positives in this project — skip any pattern/file combination you previously confirmed as false positive
   158	      1. Read your agent instructions at /Users/manuelturpin/.claude/skills/sentinel-security/agents/{agent}.md
   159	      2. Read the common execution protocol at /Users/manuelturpin/.claude/skills/sentinel-security/agents/_protocol.md
   160	      3. Audit the project at {target_path} following your Execution Protocol
   161	      4. Use Read + Grep + Bash for KB pattern scanning (read rules.json, grep patterns, enrich via RAG)
   162	      5. Only use MCP tools if your agent lists them (scan-dependencies for supply-chain, scan-headers for web/cors/ssl/static) — MCP tools are inherited automatically from the parent session (v2.1.101+)
   163	      6. Return ONLY a JSON code block containing a Finding[] array — no other text",
   164	    memory: "project",
   165	    disallowedTools: ["Write", "Edit", "NotebookEdit"],
   166	    maxTurns: 15,
   167	    run_in_background: true
   168	  )
   169	  // Monitor agent progress: Monitor("{agent}") — streams stdout events as notifications
   170	  // When agent completes: TaskUpdate(status: "completed", summary: "{N} findings")
   171	  // Named agents can coordinate: SendMessage(to: "web-audit", message: "compromised dep found in lodash")
   172	```
   173	
   174	### Step 2b: Resilience Hooks
   175	
   176	**PostCompact hook** — long multi-agent audits may trigger context compaction. Configure a PostCompact hook to reinject critical scan state after compaction:
   177	- List of dispatched agents and their status (running/completed/failed)
   178	- Current finding count per agent
   179	- Scan metadata (target path, detected stacks, start time)
   180	
   181	**StopFailure hook** — if an agent fails due to API error (rate limit, timeout), the StopFailure hook should:
   182	1. Log the failure: agent name, error type, turn count at failure
   183	2. Retry the agent once with `maxTurns: 10` (reduced scope)
   184	3. If retry fails, mark the agent as failed and continue — do NOT block the entire audit
   185	
   186	### Step 2c: Workflow-Based Orchestration (v2.1.154+)
   187	
   188	For large monorepos or wide agent fan-outs, the dynamic **Workflow tool** can orchestrate the audit deterministically instead of manual `Agent` dispatch. Author a workflow (or call `Workflow` directly) that fans the detected agents out as a `pipeline`, then adversarially verifies each finding before aggregation:
   189	
   190	```js
   191	// one stage per detected agent, then a verify pass per finding (no barrier between agents)
   192	const findings = await pipeline(
   193	  detectedAgents,                                   // e.g. [web-audit, api-audit, ...]
   194	  a => agent(a.prompt, { phase: 'Scan', schema: FINDINGS }),
   195	  scan => parallel(scan.findings.map(f => () =>
   196	    agent(`Adversarially verify: ${f.title}`, { phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICT })
   197	      .then(v => ({ ...f, verdict: v }))))
   198	)
   199	```
   200	
   201	Benefits: background execution across tens-to-hundreds of agents, built-in worktree isolation per stage, and per-finding adversarial verification that drops false positives before they reach the SARIF report. The manual Step 2 dispatch (`run_in_background` + Monitor) remains the fallback for small projects or when a deterministic script is overkill.
   202	
   203	### Step 3: Collect & Parse Results
   204	
   205	Wait for all agents to complete. For each agent result:
   206	
   207	1. **Extract JSON**: Parse the agent's response to find the JSON code block (between ` ```json ` and ` ``` ` or `[` to `]`)
   208	2. **Validate findings against schema**: Each finding MUST conform to this JSON schema — reject any finding missing required fields:
   209	   - **Required**: `id` (string), `severity` (CRITICAL|HIGH|MEDIUM|LOW|INFO), `title` (string), `description` (string), `location.file` (string), `remediation` (string)
   210	   - **Optional**: `location.line`, `location.column`, `standard`, `owasp`, `cwe`, `cvss_v4` (number 0-10), `epss` (number 0-1)
   211	   - Discard findings that don't match (log which ones were rejected and why)
   212	3. **Fallback**: If an agent does not return valid JSON:
   213	   - Log a warning: `"WARNING: Agent {agent_name} did not return valid Finding[] JSON — skipping"`
   214	   - Continue with remaining agents — do NOT fail the entire audit
   215	4. **Tag findings**: Add the originating agent name to each finding for traceability
   216	5. **Track agent success**: Count successful vs failed agents for the report summary
   217	
   218	**Edge cases:**
   219	- **0 agents dispatched** (only defaults run): Proceed with `supply-chain-audit` and `data-privacy-audit` only. Add a note in the report: "No stack-specific agents detected — only default agents ran."
   220	- **All agents fail**: Generate a minimal error report with 0 findings, listing which agents failed and why. Do NOT return an empty response — always produce a report.
   221	
   222	### Step 4: Aggregate & Score
   223	
   224	1. **Merge** all agent findings into a unified array
   225	2. **Deduplicate** findings by `location.file` + `location.line` + `id` — keep the finding with the higher `cvss_v4` score
   226	3. **Calculate risk scores**:
   227	   - **CVSS v4** base score from the finding (set by agent from KB enrichment)
   228	   - **EPSS** probability if CVE is mapped (set by agent from KB enrichment)
   229	   - **Composite risk** = `cvss_v4 * (0.6 + 0.4 * epss)` — EPSS boosts score up to 40% max (see `risk-scorer.ts`)
   230	4. **Sort** findings by composite risk (highest first)
   231	
   232	### Step 5: Generate Report
   233	
   234	Use the report renderer (`report-renderer.ts`) with the template at `/Users/manuelturpin/.sentinel/reports/templates/full-report.md`:
   235	
   236	1. Build a `ReportData` object from the aggregated findings, scan metadata (stacks, agents, depth, duration), and file paths
   237	2. Include agent success summary: "X of Y agents completed successfully" in the report header
   238	3. Call `renderReport(data)` to produce the final Markdown report
   239	4. The renderer handles severity counts, composite scores, EPSS averages, and finding categorization automatically
   240	
   241	### Step 6: Save Reports
   242	
   243	Save all 3 output files to `/Users/manuelturpin/.sentinel/reports/archive/`:
   244	
   245	1. `{project}_{date}.sarif.json` — SARIF 2.1.0 report (with `invocations` and `artifacts`)
   246	2. `{project}_{date}.sbom.json` — CycloneDX 1.5 SBOM (from `generate-sbom` tool)
   247	3. `{project}_{date}.md` — Rendered Markdown report
   248	
   249	### Step 7: Suggest Batch Remediation (Optional)
   250	
   251	After presenting the report, check if multiple findings share the same remediation pattern (e.g., 5+ files missing a security header, or 5+ endpoints without input validation). If so, suggest:
   252	
   253	> "I found {N} files with the same issue ({pattern}). You can use `/batch` to apply the fix across all files in parallel — want me to set that up?"
   254	
   255	This uses Claude Code's `/batch` skill which creates parallel worktrees for safe concurrent modifications.
   256	
   257	### Step 8: Cross-Validation (Optional)
   258	
   259	For CRITICAL/HIGH findings only, the model can now invoke the built-in `/security-review` skill as a second-pass validation (v2.1.108+ — built-in slash commands discoverable via Skill tool):
   260	
   261	```
   262	Skill("security-review")
   263	```
   264	
   265	This provides an independent review using Anthropic's official threat model. Use it when:
   266	- A CRITICAL finding could be a false positive (high-cost remediation)
   267	- The user explicitly requests independent verification
   268	- Before opening a GitHub Advisory
   269	
   270	For large-scale validation across the whole branch, invoke `/code-review ultra` — the deep multi-agent cloud review that runs parallel analysis + critique. (The `/simplify` command was renamed to `/code-review` with optional effort levels — e.g. `/code-review high` — in v2.1.146; `/ultrareview` still works as the alias for the ultra tier.)
   271	
   272	---
   273	
   274	## Knowledge Base Integration
   275	
   276	Agents now read the Knowledge Base directly using native tools (Read, Grep, Bash) instead of MCP calls. This eliminates MCP serialization overhead for local operations.
   277	
   278	**Direct KB access paths:**
   279	- **Rules**: `/Users/manuelturpin/.sentinel/knowledge-base/domains/{domain}/rules.json` — agents Read these directly and Grep each rule's `detect.patterns[]` against the project
   280	- **Standards**: `/Users/manuelturpin/.sentinel/knowledge-base/standards/` — cross-reference for standard mappings
   430	```
   431	
   432	This returns a pre-configured answer via `updatedInput`, enabling fully unattended audit runs without interactive prompts.
   433	
   434	## CI/Headless Mode
   435	
   436	When invoked via `claude -p`, produce structured SARIF output:
   437	
   438	1. Detect headless mode: if no interactive terminal is available, skip Markdown rendering
   439	2. Output the SARIF 2.1.0 JSON directly to stdout (no wrapping, no prose)
   440	3. Exit with code 0 (clean) or 1 (findings with CRITICAL/HIGH severity)
   441	
   442	**Sandbox hardening** — for enterprise CI, set `sandbox.failIfUnavailable: true` in settings to ensure audits fail-closed if the sandbox cannot start (v2.1.83+). Security-critical scans must never run unsandboxed:
   443	```json
   444	{ "sandbox": { "failIfUnavailable": true } }
   445	```
   446	
   447	**Use `--bare` flag** for faster CI cold starts — skips hooks, LSP, and plugin sync:
   448	```bash
   449	# MCP_CONNECTION_NONBLOCKING skips MCP connection wait (v2.1.89+)
   450	# CLAUDE_CODE_SCRIPT_CAPS limits script invocations (v2.1.98+)
   451	# --exclude-dynamic-system-prompt-sections improves cross-user prompt caching (v2.1.98+)
   452	CLAUDE_CODE_SCRIPT_CAPS=500 MCP_CONNECTION_NONBLOCKING=true claude --bare -p "/sentinel-security" --output-format json --exclude-dynamic-system-prompt-sections > report.sarif.json
   453	```
   454	
   455	**Resume deferred audits by name** — if a CI audit was paused via `defer` hook decision, resume it using the session title instead of opaque ID (v2.1.101+):
   456	```bash
   457	claude -p --resume "sentinel-audit-myproject-2026-04-12"
   458	```
   459	
   460	**Deep link for one-click audit launch** (v2.1.91 multi-line support):
   461	```
   462	claude-cli://open?q=/sentinel-security%0A%0APerform%20a%20full%20security%20audit%20of%20this%20project.%0ASeverity%20threshold:%20MEDIUM%0ADepth:%20standard
   463	```
   464	Share this link in README, Slack, or docs for instant audit launch from anywhere. Multi-line prompts via `%0A` encoding let you pre-fill audit parameters.
   465	
   466	**Use `--json-schema` for guaranteed valid SARIF** — enforces output structure at the model level, eliminating JSON parse failures in CI:
   467	```bash
   468	claude --bare -p "/sentinel-security" --json-schema '{"type":"object","properties":{"$schema":{"type":"string"},"version":{"type":"string"},"runs":{"type":"array"}},"required":["version","runs"]}' > report.sarif.json
   469	```
   470	
   471	## Reactive Re-scan (FileChanged Hook)
   472	
   473	For continuous security monitoring during development, configure a **FileChanged hook** to trigger a lightweight re-scan when security-sensitive files change:
   474	
   475	Watched patterns: `*.env`, `**/auth/**`, `**/middleware/**`, `docker-compose.*`, `Dockerfile`, `*.tf`, `package.json`, `requirements.txt`
   476	
   477	When triggered:
   478	1. Run only the relevant agent(s) for the changed file type (not a full scan)
   479	2. Compare findings against the last full scan — report only **new** findings
   480	3. Display inline: `"[Sentinel] New finding: {title} in {file}:{line}"`
   481	
   482	This uses Claude Code's `CwdChanged` and `FileChanged` hook events (v2.1.83+).
   483	
   484	## HTTP Hook Notifications
   485	
   486	Configure an **HTTP hook** to POST scan results to Slack or a webhook endpoint when an audit completes. This enables async notification for long-running scans.
   487	
   488	**Setup** (in `~/.claude/settings.json`):
   489	```json
   490	{
   491	  "hooks": {
   492	    "TaskCompleted": [{
   493	      "matcher": "sentinel-security",
   494	      "hooks": [{
   495	        "type": "http",
   496	        "url": "https://hooks.slack.com/services/YOUR/WEBHOOK/URL",
   497	        "method": "POST",
   498	        "body": {
   499	          "text": "Sentinel scan complete: {{task.summary}}"
   500	        }
   501	      }]
   502	    }]
   503	  }
   504	}
   505	```
   506	
   507	This uses Claude Code's HTTP hooks (v2.1.63+). Configure the webhook URL in `.sentinel.json` or environment variable `SENTINEL_WEBHOOK_URL`.
   508	
   509	## Auto-Redeploy (ConfigChange Hook)
   510	
   511	Configure a **ConfigChange hook** to auto-trigger `deploy.sh` when KB rules or skill files change:
   512	
   513	Watched paths: `knowledge-base/domains/*/rules.json`, `skills/security/SKILL.md`, `skills/security/agents/*.md`
   514	
   515	When triggered:
   516	1. Run `DRY_RUN=0 bash scripts/deploy.sh` to sync changes to `~/.claude/skills/sentinel-security/` and `~/.sentinel/` — **`DRY_RUN=0` is mandatory**: the script defaults to `DRY_RUN=1` since 2026-04-17 (H3 audit fix), so the bare `bash scripts/deploy.sh` is a silent no-op. This hook has been dead since that change. *(Finding S1b de la revue croisée du 27/07.)*
   517	2. Log the deploy result
   518	
   519	This uses Claude Code's ConfigChange hook event (v2.1.50+) and eliminates forgetting to run `deploy.sh` after edits.
   520	
   521	## WorktreeCreate/Remove Hooks (Agent Lifecycle Tracking)
   522	
   523	When agents run with `isolation: worktree`, configure **WorktreeCreate/Remove hooks** for audit trail and lifecycle tracking:
   524	
   525	```json
   526	{
   527	  "hooks": {
   528	    "WorktreeCreate": [{
   529	      "hooks": [{
   530	        "type": "command",
   531	        "command": "echo \"$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ) WORKTREE_CREATE: $WORKTREE_PATH\" >> ~/.sentinel/reports/audit-trail.log"
   532	      }]
   533	    }],
   534	    "WorktreeRemove": [{
   535	      "hooks": [{
   536	        "type": "command",
   537	        "command": "echo \"$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ) WORKTREE_REMOVE: $WORKTREE_PATH\" >> ~/.sentinel/reports/audit-trail.log"
   538	      }]
   539	    }]
   540	  }
   541	}
   542	```
   543	
   544	This creates a timestamped record of every isolated agent workspace — useful for compliance audits (SOC-2, ISO 27001) and debugging orphaned worktrees.
   545	
   546	**Stale worktree auto-cleanup** (v2.1.105+) — worktrees from agents whose PR was squash-merged are now automatically removed instead of lingering indefinitely. This reduces disk usage for Sentinel's worktree-heavy multi-agent workflow without manual cleanup.
   547	
   548	## OTEL Distributed Tracing (Audit Performance)
   549	
   550	For audit performance observability, enable OpenTelemetry tracing with fine-grained controls (v2.1.98+):
   551	
   552	```bash
   553	# Enable TRACEPARENT propagation to Bash subprocesses
   554	export OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT="http://localhost:4318"
   555	# Fine-grained controls (v2.1.101+)
   556	export OTEL_LOG_TOOL_DETAILS=1    # Log tool parameters in spans
   557	export OTEL_LOG_TOOL_CONTENT=1    # Log tool results in spans
   558	```
   559	
   560	When enabled, each agent dispatch, KB query, and RAG enrichment call gets a proper span in the trace tree. Use this to identify:
   561	- Slow agents (high turn count or large context)
   562	- Expensive KB queries (RAG latency)
   563	- MCP tool bottlenecks (scan-dependencies, scan-headers)
   564	
   565	## Important Notes
   566	
   567	- Never expose secrets or credentials found during scanning — redact them in reports
   568	- Rate findings conservatively: only mark as CRITICAL if exploitation is trivial and impact is severe
   569	- Provide actionable remediations, not just descriptions of problems
   570	- When unsure about a finding's severity, consult the CVSS v4 calculator rules
   571	- Always run supply-chain-audit regardless of stack — every project has dependencies
   572	
   573	## Plugin Distribution
   574	
   575	Sentinel can be packaged as a distributable Claude Code plugin for one-command install:
   576	
   577	```
   578	sentinel-plugin/
   579	  package.json          — plugin manifest (name, version, skills, agents, mcp-servers, bin)
   580	  bin/                  — executable commands (v2.1.91+ plugin bin/ support)
   581	    sentinel-cve-sync   — sync CVE feeds from NVD/OSV/GitHub
   582	    sentinel-anthropic-sync — sync Anthropic ecosystem releases
   583	    sentinel-index      — re-index all ChromaDB knowledge bases
   584	  skills/
   585	    sentinel-security/  — SKILL.md + agents/
   586	    sentinel-rag/       — SKILL.md + knowledge/
   587	    sentinel-evolve/    — SKILL.md + knowledge/
   588	  mcp-servers/
   589	    sentinel-scanner/   — TypeScript MCP server (scan-dependencies, scan-headers)
   590	  knowledge-base/       — rules, standards, CVE feeds
   591	  rag/                  — ChromaDB indexer, query, config
   592	  scripts/              — deploy, sync, cron scripts
   593	```
   594	
   595	Plugin `bin/` executables (v2.1.91+) are available as bare commands from the Bash tool after install — no path required:
   596	```bash
   597	sentinel-cve-sync --days 90
   598	sentinel-anthropic-sync
   599	sentinel-index
   600	```
   601	
   602	Users install with: `claude plugin install sentinel` — this deploys all skills, agents, MCP server, and KB automatically.
   603	
   604	**Offline environments** — set `CLAUDE_CODE_PLUGIN_KEEP_MARKETPLACE_ON_FAILURE=1` to preserve the local marketplace cache when `git pull` fails (v2.1.90+). This prevents plugin install failures in air-gapped or restricted network environments.
   605	
   606	**Enterprise fail-closed policy** — for enterprise deployments, enable `forceRemoteSettingsRefresh` in managed settings (v2.1.92+). This blocks CLI startup until remote managed settings are freshly fetched — if the fetch fails, the CLI exits instead of running with stale or missing policy. This ensures Sentinel audits always run under the latest org security policy.
   607	
   608	## Mode: evolve
   609	
   610	**Autonomous Threat Intelligence Pipeline** — keeps Sentinel's detection rules and coverage up-to-date by syncing threat feeds, measuring coverage, and producing a Threat Intelligence Report (TIR).
   611	
   612	This mode is the security counterpart to `/sentinel-evolve` (which optimizes Claude Code tooling). `/sentinel-security evolve` focuses on **what to detect** (rules, patterns, CVEs), not **how to detect** (tools, hooks, model tiers).
   613	
   614	### Invocation
   615	
   616	```
   617	/sentinel-security evolve        # Full pipeline (sync → score → report)
   618	/sentinel-security evolve sync   # Fetch all threat intel sources
   619	/sentinel-security evolve score  # Measure coverage vs standards
   620	/sentinel-security evolve report # Generate TIR
   621	```
   622	
   623	Phase 2 (future): `evolve gen` (LLM pattern generation), `evolve test` (rule validation), `evolve feedback` (FP/TP loop).
   624	
   625	### Step 1: sync — Fetch Threat Intel Sources
   626	
   627	Run the extended sync pipeline:
   628	```
   629	Bash: python3 /Users/manuelturpin/.sentinel/scripts/cve-sync.py
   630	```
   631	
   632	This fetches from 6 sources:
   633	1. **NVD API v2** — CVE data with CVSS scores
   634	2. **OSV API** — Open source vulnerabilities (300+ packages, 6 ecosystems)
   635	3. **GitHub Advisories** — Supply chain security advisories
   636	4. **EPSS** — Exploit Prediction Scoring (probability of exploitation)
   637	5. **CISA KEV** — Known Exploited Vulnerabilities (actively exploited in the wild)
   638	6. **OWASP/CWE Standards** — Checks GitHub for new versions of OWASP Top 10 lists
   639	
   640	After sync, report:
   641	- New CVEs added per source
   642	- New KEV entries (actively exploited — highlight these)
   643	- Standards updates available (flag if OWASP released a new version)
   644	
   645	### Step 2: score — Coverage Measurement
   646	
   647	Run the coverage scorer:
   648	```
   649	Bash: python3 /Users/manuelturpin/.sentinel/scripts/coverage-scorer.py --json
   650	```
   651	
   652	Parse the JSON output and present:
   653	- Coverage % for each standard (OWASP Web, API, LLM, Mobile, CWE-25, MITRE ATLAS)
   654	- Gaps: uncovered standard items with their IDs and names
   655	- Rule quality: manual vs auto-with-patterns vs auto-template-only
   656	
   657	### Step 3: report — Threat Intelligence Report (TIR)
   658	
   659	Generate the TIR using the template at `/Users/manuelturpin/.sentinel/reports/templates/threat-intel-report.md`.
   660	
   661	Fill in all placeholders with data from Steps 1-2:
   662	1. Sync summary (per-source status and counts)
   663	2. Rule quality breakdown
   664	3. Standards coverage table with grades (OK >= 90%, WARN >= 70%, GAP < 70%)
   665	4. Gap analysis (uncovered items per standard)
   666	5. KEV highlights (most critical actively-exploited vulns)
   667	6. Recommendations (based on gaps and new threats)
   668	
   669	Save to `/Users/manuelturpin/.sentinel/reports/archive/TIR-{date}.md` and `/Users/manuelturpin/.sentinel/reports/archive/TIR-{date}.json`.
   670	
   671	### Cron Schedule
   672	
   673	The evolve pipeline is designed for weekly automated runs:
   674	```
   675	Weekly  Mon 6:00 AM  — cve-sync.py (all 6 sources)
   676	Weekly  Mon 9:00 AM  — coverage-scorer.py + TIR generation
   677	```
   678	
   679	For manual runs, invoke `/sentinel-security evolve` anytime.
   680	
   681	---
   682	
   683	## Multi-Skill Orchestration (Agent Teams)
   684	
   685	Sentinel skills can be orchestrated as an **agent team** with a shared task board for self-improving audit loops.
   686	
   687	### Setup
   688	
   689	```
   690	TeamCreate("sentinel", members: ["sentinel-security", "sentinel-rag", "sentinel-evolve"])
   691	```
   692	
   693	### Roles
   694	
   695	| Member | Role | Responsibilities |
   696	|--------|------|-----------------|
   697	| **sentinel-security** | Team lead | Orchestrates scans, dispatches agents, aggregates findings |
   698	| **sentinel-rag** | KB expert | Enriches findings via RAG, diagnoses KB gaps, optimizes search quality |
   699	| **sentinel-evolve** | Intel analyst | Monitors Claude Code updates, suggests skill improvements |
   700	
   701	### Self-Improving Scan Loop
   702	
   703	When the team is active, the audit workflow extends with a closed feedback loop:
   704	
   705	1. **Scan** — sentinel-security runs a full audit, producing findings
   706	2. **Analyze gaps** — if findings reference CVE patterns not in the KB, SendMessage to sentinel-rag: `"New pattern detected: {pattern}. Check if KB has coverage."`
   707	3. **Update KB** — sentinel-rag adds missing rules/patterns to the KB and re-indexes
   708	4. **Re-scan** — sentinel-security re-runs only the affected agents with updated KB
   709	5. **Evolve** — sentinel-evolve logs the improvement in its update_history for tracking
   710	
   711	### TeammateIdle Auto-Orchestration (v2.1.33+)
   712	
   713	Configure a **TeammateIdle hook** to automate the self-improving loop handoffs:
   714	
   715	```json
   716	{
   717	  "hooks": {
   718	    "TeammateIdle": [{
   719	      "matcher": "sentinel-security",
   720	      "hooks": [{
   721	        "type": "command",
   722	        "command": "echo 'Scan agent idle — triggering KB gap analysis via sentinel-rag'"
   723	      }]
   724	    }]
   725	  }
   726	}
   727	```
   728	
   729	When a scan agent completes and goes idle, TeammateIdle fires — the team lead (sentinel-security) then SendMessages to sentinel-rag to check for KB gaps, triggering step 2 of the self-improving loop without manual intervention.
   730	
   731	### Activation
   732	
   733	Agent teams are experimental (v2.1.32+). The team is activated on demand — add this to Step 0 when the user requests "deep scan" or "self-improving scan":
   734	
   735	```
   736	If user requests deep/self-improving scan:
   737	  TeamCreate("sentinel", members: ["sentinel-security", "sentinel-rag", "sentinel-evolve"])
   738	  // Team members can now SendMessage to each other
   739	  // Shared task board tracks cross-skill work
   740	  // TeammateIdle hook auto-triggers KB gap analysis when scan agents finish

